{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>A fire-offering, a pleasing fragrance.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Menachot 110a): It is stated concerning the animal burnt-offering ״אִשֵּׁה רֵיחַ״ and so on, and it is stated concerning the bird burnt-offering ״אִשֵּׁה״ and so on — to tell you: whether one who offers much or one who offers little, etc. — end of quotation. Now, our Rabbis of blessed memory intended to resolve what is difficult, namely that Scripture says ״אִשֵּׁה רֵיחַ נִיחֹחַ״ concerning the bird, which is worth only an issar, and all the more so concerning the animal burnt-offering. For this reason they were precise in their language and said: to tell you, whether one who offers much, etc. — meaning that Scripture comes to inform us that there is no difference between the small offering and the great one, lest you say that this is only because HaShem, from His side of mercy, is appeased by the poor man's offering even though it is not as significant as the rich man's offering — yet in any case the tangible cannot be denied, for this one brings a bull and that one brings a bird. Therefore Scripture was wise and said ״רֵיחַ נִיחֹחַ״ concerning the animal, and it was not sufficient for Him to say it concerning the bird, from which one might learn — to alert you and show you that it is no less than an animal offering, from which one might learn; rather, He needed to inform you even concerning the animal offering: ״אִשֵּׁה רֵיחַ נִיחֹחַ״. This is what the Tanna was precise about in saying 'it is stated' and 'it is stated,' etc. — meaning, it was not sufficient to state the matter at the lesser level; He intended to tell you thereby: one who offers much and one who offers little — meaning, they are equal. And therefore the Tanna did not say ״to tell that the one who offers little is like the one who offers much,״ for then the intention I explained in his words would fall away, because if he were to reach the point of saying that this is like that, he would be forced to say that the one learned from is lesser than the one teaching, like the rule of teacher and student. And from the precision of his language in saying ״one״ and ״one,״ this points with the finger of intellect that there is no rank of one above the other. And in this lies the resolution he achieved for why it did not say ״רֵיחַ נִיחֹחַ״ concerning the bird — for it comes to teach us this very thing, that the rich man has no superiority in his ox, nothing at all. And for this reason as well, it was necessary to say ״אִשֵּׁה רֵיחַ נִיחֹחַ״ concerning the flock (צֹּאן), and it was not sufficient with cattle and bird, which are two extremes, for in this matter nothing is learned from the other, for the reason brought above."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:52:36.427930006Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}